Picture \par
\par
Who giveth this woman to be married?\rquote  In an age when feminism has stressed female independence and control, a modern girl might well ask: \lquote How can anyone give a woman?\rquote  However, in the marriage ceremony in the Book of Common Prayer which was in use until twentieth-century reforms, the officiating minister asked this question. At this point the bride\rquote s father, who had led her to church, offered her hand to the priest, who in turn gave her hand to her future husband. In other words the bride was her father\rquote s to give \f1\emdash  he gave her into the care of the church and the church gave her to the bridegroom. She was a piece of property. \par
\par
We still ask who is going to \lquote give away\rquote  a bride. Conventionally a father could refuse permission to marry if he thought the prospective husband did not have the financial means to support his daughter, but parental wisdom and youthful passion do not always concur. A young woman \emdash  particularly in works of literature \emdash  might defy a man\rquote s right to control her in this way. \par
\par
Marriage and money \par
\par
In Romeo and Juliet, for instance, Juliet\rquote s father chooses Paris for her husband. Nothing in the play leads us to believe that Paris would be a bad husband: he is clearly devoted to Juliet, is wealthy and titled, and admired by the family. However, Juliet, a passionate and strong-willed teenager, falls in love with an entirely unsuitable young man who belongs to a family that has long been feuding with her people. Arguments of duty and obedience carry no weight with the passionate girl so that tragedy ensues. \par
\par
A similar situation arises in The Changeling by Thomas Middleton and William Rowley. Vermandero chooses a good and wealthy man to marry his daughter. We have no cause to think that Alonzo de Piracquo would be anything other than a kind and loving husband, and until she sets eyes on Alsemero, Beatrice-Joanna accepts as much. Once subject to physical desire, she is prepared to go to any lengths to have her own way. We have much less sympathy for her than for Juliet because she is prepared to stoop to murder to achieve her ends. \par
\par
Essential to both plots is the fact that the father has total control over whom his daughter marries, and she has no legal redress if he stands firm. We may ask why both fathers are so intransigent. Why cannot these fathers allow their daughters to follow their hearts? We may answer this question in two words: honour and property. There is no mention of a brother for either Juliet or Beatrice-Joanna, so presumably they are only children and therefore likely to inherit the family property. The honourable name of the family has to be maintained by marriage into a family of similar social standing. If possible the family estate should be augmented by a marriage which unites it with similar wealth. Such was the plight of single girls in families of high social standing in early modern England. \par
\par
A modern audience finds it much easier to sympathise with young women subjected to such conventions and deprived of free will in the matter of love and marriage, though even today, in the case of dynastic marriages, wealth and status are significant factors. Capulet and Vermandero are both heads of distinguished families, and guided by concerns about reputation and social consequence. Presumably when the Duke of Calabria \emdash  the father of Ferdinand, the Cardinal, and the Duchess \emdash  married his daughter to the Duke of Malfi, such considerations would have weighed with him. \par
\par
Remarrying widows \par
\par
But what would be the case had Juliet married Paris and become a countess, or Beatrice-Joanna taken her noble husband\rquote s title, and they were then widowed? Wealthy husbands were often older than their wives, and likely to predecease them, while younger husbands risked being killed in war. The rate of remarriage in sixteenth- and seventeenth-century England was similar to what it is now, though the reason was death rather than divorce. If the husband died, unless the land and money had been specifically willed away from her, his widow was beholden to no one, particularly if she was childless. If women could acquire financial security through marriage, they could also find power in widowhood. There was no father or husband to control her. In a male-dominated society where women\rquote s rights and horizons were distinctly limited, such independence could irritate men who believed themselves entitled to the power. John Webster chooses for his heroine a woman in this situation in The Duchess of Malfi. \par
\par
Webster gives us no hint about how or why the Duke of Malfi died. The significant fact is that the heroine is a widow, with, presumably, the widow\rquote s independence and power. She is, beyond question, wealthy. When he sets Bosola to spy on his sister, Ferdinand says he has reasons that he does not want to disclose for wanting his sister to remain unmarried. When he is filled with remorse for her murder, he says he had no reason to do what he did, except that he had: \par
\par
\emspace\emspace\emspace\emspace\emspace\emspace\emspace\emspace a hope, \par
Had she continued widow, to have gained \par
An infinite mass of treasure by her death; \emspace\emspace (IV.ii.282\endash  4) \par
\par
It is not the first time that Ferdinand refers to his sister\rquote s wealth. When he learns of her marriage, he wishes that he could be a tempest so that he \lquote might toss her palace \rquote bout her ears, / Root up her goodly forests, blast her meads, / And lay her general territory [\'85] waste\rquote  (II.v.18\endash 20). The Duchess, who has presumably inherited all these lands from the Duke, was childless by her first marriage, so that if she predeceases her brothers, they stand to gain her property on her death. \par
\par
Male relatives in this position had good reason to be disapproving of a second marriage, even if it was advantageous. Women could remarry to their profit: one of Queen Elizabeth\rquote s subjects, Elizabeth Talbot, Countess of Shrewsbury, married four times, amassing in the process an enormous fortune enabling her to build the great houses of Hardwick Hall and the first Chatsworth House. However, a second husband might outlive his wife, and might father children who would put all hope of inheritance beyond a brother\rquote s reach. Such a concern clearly troubles Ferdinand \emdash  a wealthy widow might well be preyed upon by unscrupulous young men who had insufficient means to make them acceptable to the father or brother of a young heiress still under his protection. Ferdinand, in The Duchess of Malfi, purports to have such anxieties about his wealthy sister. He and his brother believe they have been acting as her advisors, and now that they are about to leave her to her own devices, they issue warnings. Ferdinand makes quite clear her status and the dangers that, as far as he is concerned, accompany that status: \par
\par
\emspace\emspace\emspace\emspace You are a widow: \par
You know already what man is, and therefore \par
Let not youth, high promotion, eloquence \emdash  \emspace\emspace (I.i.293\endash 5) \par
\par
He would conclude \lquote seduce you into a second marriage\rquote , but he is cut short by his brother, the Cardinal: \par
\par
No, nor anything without the addition, honour, \par
Sway your high blood. (I.i.296\endash 7) \par
\par
While Ferdinand seems primarily concerned with wealth, his brother is more exercised by the idea of honour. The Cardinal is anxious that she might marry a social inferior, but Ferdinand wants to prevent any marriage, warning his sister: \lquote Marry! They are most luxurious, / Will wed twice.\rquote  (I.i.297\endash 8). The brothers proceed to elaborate on their warnings, pointing out that most widows are hastily remarried, and speak of the flattery and hypocrisy that might tempt her. Their fear seems to be that their widowed sister will be seduced by a \lquote neat knave with a smooth tale\rquote  (I.i.339). \par
\par
 \par
\par
Women and desire \par
\par
In Romeo and Juliet and The Changeling, the writers confront the uneasy issue of female sexuality and desire. The Nurse is very ready to talk about the physical implications of marriage, while Juliet is desperately eager for \lquote love-performing night\rquote  to arrive. Vermandero in The Changeling is happy to believe that it is his daughter\rquote s modesty that makes her urge a delay in the wedding plans, not her fierce desire for another man. Unmarried girls may be presumed to be chaste in every respect, but what of widows? When the Duchess is addressed as \lquote lusty widow\rquote  (I.i.340), her brother may mean bonny and healthy, but that almost certainly encompasses normal healthy desires and appetites. \par
\par
It was generally presumed that widows were eager to renew the pleasures of the marriage bed. The uneasiness in men\rquote s minds about older women\rquote s sexual urges can give rise to comedy, but also to disgust: as Ferdinand claims, \lquote women like that part which, like the lamprey / Hath ne\rquote er a bone in \rquote t\rquote  (I.i.336\endash 7). Much has been made in modern critical analysis of the psychological root of Hamlet\rquote s revulsion at the second marriage of his mother, Queen Gertrude. Her marriage to Hamlet\rquote s uncle may well have stood in the way of Hamlet\rquote s gaining the crown, he may suspect his uncle\rquote s foul play, but he seems to be more concerned about the horror of his mother\rquote s sexual desire. As he envisages the king\rquote s \lquote reechy kisses\rquote , or his \lquote paddling in your neck with his damned fingers\rquote , we sense Hamlet\rquote s physical revulsion at the idea of this older woman living \lquote in the rank sweat of an enseamed bed / Stewed in corruption, honeying and making love / Over the nasty sty\rquote . Similarly Ferdinand\rquote s insane violence, triggered by news of her marriage, hints at the incestuous passion which lurks beneath his concerns about her property, as his fantasy of seeing her \lquote in the shameful act of sin / [\'85] with some strong-thighed bargeman\rquote  suggests (II.v.41\endash 2). \par
\par
Love and marriage \par
\par
Both Juliet and Beatrice-Joanna take an active role in wooing, driven to it by the restrictions of parental power. The Duchess has to take the initiative for a different reason: the Duchess has fallen in love with a man of lower station, an honest, intelligent and trustworthy man, the steward of her household (the same position as that occupied by Malvolio in Twelfth Night). The Duke had approved of him as a servant, but could never have countenanced the idea of Antonio\rquote s marriage to a woman of ducal rank: he has neither property nor title to recommend him. The Duchess herself is aware of the difference in status between her and the man she loves. Because she is wealthy and socially superior, the Duchess cannot expect Antonio to court her. It is \lquote The misery of us that are born great \emdash  / We are forced to woo, because none dare woo us\rquote  (I.i.441\endash 2). \par
\par
She declares her love as \lquote a young widow / That claims you for her husband, and, like a widow, / Iuse but half a blush in \rquote t\rquote  (I.i.457\endash 9). \par
\par
Her right to choose her husband is beyond question, and she hopes, ultimately, to appease the hostility towards it of her brothers, who have no legal power to prevent their sister from remarrying. However, because the Duchess knows her brothers disapprove of a second marriage, this one is informally sworn in a private room, not conducted as a public ceremony. We might well consider, though, that this secret declaration carries more moral weight than rites conducted in church by such a corrupt figure as the Cardinal. She also tries to hide the birth of her children, though Bosola discovers her secret. Her marriage and motherhood are not stained by ambition or greed \emdash  she uses her independence to sustain all that is wholesome in human relationships. Her love for her husband and children, however, renders her vulnerable. \par
\par
Webster is able to present in this wealthy, experienced woman a foil to the evil and decadence of the court. Her marriage may not be conventional and its legality may be questionable, but it is a truer marriage than the faithless Julia\rquote s \emdash  her love is more innocent and pure than the lascivious desires of the Cardinal. She may be a wealthy noblewoman, but her true greatness is revealed when, in her dying moments, she asks Cariola to give her \lquote little boy / Some syrup for his cold\rquote  and to make sure that her little girl says her prayers before going to sleep (IV.ii.203\endash  4). The emphasis is on the physical and spiritual health of her children, not juvenile self-interest. Shakespeare\rquote s and Middleton\rquote s young women die childless, leaving the futile grief of well-intentioned fathers. The Duchess, a mature married woman, leaves an heir, a \lquote young hopeful gentleman\rquote  who will be established \lquote In\rquote s mother\rquote s right\rquote  (V.v.112\endash 13), and a noble name that will outlive the evil of the play. \par
\par
